The source ranking is now decisive: the high-pressure CaWO₄ fergusonite model is grossly incompatible (Rwp ≈46%), while hexagonal/tetragonal WO₃ alternatives remain at ≈17.5–19.2%. The passing monoclinic WO₃ source is already at ≈14.0% in the same fixed-coordinate model; I’m finishing the near-monoclinic/triclinic comparisons, where structural and physical-screen distinctions matter most.

The nuisance tests are exposing the unstable branches cleanly: simultaneous zero + displacement is 99.9% correlated; SH/L and U run to bounds; two-parameter roughness improves Rwp but its A/B terms are 99.4% correlated. I will not retain those forms. Background restarts (6 vs 10 coefficients) change Rwp by only ~0.06%, supporting the eight-term base. Phase broadening tests are still running.

A profile restart revealed an important GSAS-II default: nominally “unrefined” phase size (1 µm) and microstrain (1000 ppm) still broaden the calculated peaks. I’m rebuilding the serious-source comparison with those effects explicitly neutralized (very large size, zero strain) and with the unsupported Caglioti U term fixed at zero. This is the required second nuisance branch, not an assumption from the training generator.

The explicit no-broadening restart confirms a stable simplification: U contributes nothing, 6/8/10 background terms give essentially the same fit, and the jointly refined roughness form again degenerates. A shift-only calibration branch is stable and marginally better than zero-only, while zero+shift remains >99.8% correlated. Polarization now refines without an immediate bound warning and gives a substantial, interpretable high-angle intensity improvement; I’ll verify its value and restart survival before retaining it.

Polarization and one-parameter roughness describe much of the same angle-dependent intensity trend: combining them yields only 0.003% Rwp improvement and a 95.9% scale/roughness correlation, so I’m keeping the simpler, better-fitting roughness branch and dropping refined polarization. A [001] March–Dollase term for monoclinic WO₃ is strongly axis-specific (the [010]/[100]/[110] controls do not improve comparably), so I’m now checking whether that texture signal survives the roughness/background restart.

The stable nuisance model now has: one-parameter surface roughness, WO₃ [001] texture, WO₃ isotropic size broadening, and a shift-only specimen-position model. Each survives background/profile restarts; adding strain after size changes Rwp by only 0.002% and makes size/strain ~89% correlated, so strain is excluded. I’m moving to the final permitted step: selective coordinate tests, beginning with CaWO₄ oxygen and WO₃ tungsten sublattices independently, followed by physical screening.

